## How I decided
1. my #2 ("do not rely on their not coming; rely on having what cannot be attacked"): I asked of each piece, what check would still hold if the builder wanted to satisfy it and not the requirement. Whatever survives only because the builder would not do that is the good-character residue.
2. my #12 ("seek it in 勢, not demand it of people"; "choose people and rely on 勢"): the fix is to move the ground — a different fixture, a held-out case, an instrumented runner — not to ask the builder to be careful.
3. my #1 (lay the matter out by the five and the seven; the chain 度→量→數→稱→勝): I compared each piece's Fails-if line (the reckoning) against what the check measures. Where the round's MET is measured by something other than the round's Fails-if (R1's coupling to map/chain/tests/callers; R1/R3 marking every requirement NOT MET), the count is of the wrong thing.
4. my #7 ("attack where they are unprepared"; "these victories cannot be handed down in advance") and my #10 ("avoid the solid, strike the empty"): the builder reads the frozen tests and the evaluator; P3 holds back no case, so the worker can see the whole check. That is not good character, it is a target.
5. my #11 ("foreknowledge ... must be taken from people who know"): the only non-leaking foreknowledge is the P4 answer keys kept from builders; before P4, every evaluator fixture is shared with the worker.
6. my #16 ("if orders are clear and still not followed, it is the officers' fault"; care without command is useless): the acceptance condition must be stated before the attempt and then enforced; `unclear`, `dynamic`, `undecided` and `{reason:"other"}` are legal escapes with no check on their use, so the order is not enforced.
7. my #15 (the general's faults, each a handle for the enemy): listed the escapes above as the worker's handles — `unclear`, `dynamic`/`unresolved`, `undecided`, `other`, and NOT CHECKED on a missing `unshare`.
8. my #13 ("commands of the ruler not accepted"): I did not accept the brief's own acceptance list as given; I checked whether "the evaluator reports MET" is a check on the requirement, and where it is not, I said so.
9. my reading (no item): the one thing no check covers is the strength of the frozen tests — sha256 proves they did not change, not that they could fail. The brief states they are not yet written, so this is untested, not verified.
10. my reading (no item): I treat "the worker" as the builder whose `src/` the round judges; where the reliance falls on Codex (test strength) or the COO (freeze, plants), I named that instead.

## What I need to know
- Does P3 get any held-out case (an answer key the builder cannot read), or does all held-out checking start in P4? If none, all eight verdicts weaken to "guarded by the builder's character".
- Do the frozen tests have a recorded pre-build failing run? If yes, item 9 narrows; if no, the tests' strength is unverified for all eight.
- Does the evaluator run its full command table against the marker fixture, or only map/chain/tests/callers? If the full table, R1 narrows.
- Is `unshare` guaranteed on the build host? If not, R3's check can silently not exist and the round can still advance.
- May a round be marked MET when the evaluator reports only NOT CHECKED parts (A5, A6, L2)? If yes, "MET" and "checked" differ and the loop's stop condition needs restating.

## Costs and risks
- Adding held-out cases (my #11) costs the builder fast feedback and can fail the round for an interface reason not in the spec; it is the price of a check the worker cannot target.
- Adding same-name / unconventional-test-path fixtures (A2, R2) costs evaluator work and new plant verification; without them the checks stay satisfiable by shape, not mechanism.
- Requiring a limit to be absent (L1) and a sibling status to be non-`unclear` (A1) risks false NOT MET on genuinely hard inputs; each needs its own fixture and its own plant, or it becomes a check that punishes honesty.
- Instrumenting the runner to catch socket/DNS attempts and `$TMPDIR` writes (R3) adds an evaluator dependency or a wrapper; the spec forbids new runtime dependencies for the tool, not for the evaluator, but it is still work and can produce false failures.
- Calling A5 not loop-ready delays that requirement to P6; that costs schedule, and the alternative costs a false MET.
- The deeper risk (my #1): a check that measures the wrong thing (R1's coupling) will spend all three attempts on map/chain/tests/callers while the round says R1, and the attempt limit will close the round on a fault the round did not name.

